use 'math_ops.dewy'

# Boundary values occupy one register. Even words encode signed 63-bit
# integers; odd words own an immutable BigInt allocation. Word proofs keep
# nonescaping locals untagged and convert only at these boundaries.
# Existing scalar domains prove narrow inputs fit the immediate payload.
# Local/range upper bounds are demanded only at an actual tagged boundary.
# (bounds proven to be words: a counter's loop over [i..i + 8) stays in words too)
# Unknown bounds may themselves have promoted. Keep the hidden counter exact;
# changing the exposed loop variable never changes the range's progression.
# Local representation proofs are scoped to an unmodified bounded induction
# variable. Semantic types and escaping values still use the stable boundary.
# Immutable captured ints with a proven signed-word domain use a raw word in
# the private environment. Selection happens once at ordinary environment
# layout; generic shared layouts retain their established representation.
# The local-indexed table and cached initializer bounds cost O(captures +
# demanded initializer nodes), with no scan of unrelated function bodies.
math_word_captures: {int: bool}
math_word_regs: {int: int}

# Nonescaping int locals with finite source widths use a vector of registers.
# Demand starts at a declaration; its indexed writes must preserve the initial
# width. No growing recurrence is guessed. Expression traversal has a fixed
# depth budget, and width is capped at four words; other values stay tagged.
math_static_regs: {int: int[]}
math_static_widths: {int: int}

# A bounded reset guard observes only whether a fresh exact value is outside
# an interval of words. An overflow can use the interval's upper endpoint as
# a temporary representative: either value selects the reset arm. The sole
# write replaces it with a word before any other use can observe it.
# Demand is one nonescaping declaration; the existing local-indexed writes
# require one reset. At most eight following statements / 128 syntax nodes
# are inspected, O(1) bounded work per candidate and no whole-body read scan.
# `x + k` compared with y, all words but the sum perhaps not: the sum in a word with its
# overflow flag. A sum past the largest word is greater than any word, and one past the
# smallest is less, so the comparison stays exact. -1 when e is not of that shape.
# Structural representation proofs, deliberately limited to one local and its
# lexical suffix. A local cannot escape by address/capture. Every write is
# inspected; nested-loop updates and multiple increment sites are rejected.
math_word_limits: {int: int}
math_word_updates: {int: bool}

# Build the write index only when a mutable mathematical local demands it.
# Loop ancestry is relative to each declaration, including declarations inside
# an outer loop; sibling scopes cannot refer to that declaration.
math_writes_ready = false
math_has_mutable = false
math_writes: {int: (int, int)[]}
math_decl_depth: {int: int}
math_loop_sites: {int: int}
math_write_loop: {int: int}     # a write of a mathematical local -> its innermost loop (0: none)
math_loop_parent: {int: int}    # a loop -> the loop it is in (0: none)

# A counter stepped up only in loops it guards: `loop i + k <? E { ... i += s }`, one
# update directly in each loop (several loops may share the counter: a word-at-a-time scan,
# then its tail; a token's loop inside a line's). The guard held at the start of the
# iteration; before the update only loops nested in this one changed i, so after it
# i <= max(E - k - 1, what those left) + s. From a nonnegative start, i stays in
# [0, the largest such bound]. O(writes x depth).
# the largest value of l after update w (in its loop's guard, after the updates in loops
# nested in that loop), or -1
# is loop a nested (at any depth) in loop b, and not b itself?
# The largest value of local l for which loop condition c can hold, or -1 when c does not
# bound l from above: `l + k <? E` or `l + k <=? E` (k a nonnegative literal, E with a
# nonnegative bound), or a conjunction with one such side.
# Prove a word invariant from the indexed writes, including resetting counters:
# i = if i < limit i+1 else 0. Each update is guarded at the update itself, so
# multiple writes and nested loops are safe. O(written expression nodes), with
# no scan of unrelated statements and no assumptions about escaping locals.
# Prove [0, limit] inductive over every indexed write. Hypothetical bounds
# remain on this recursion's stack, never in the shared expression caches.
# O(written expression edges x loop depth), bounded to 32 expression levels.
# A strict comparison against any word establishes room for subtracting one,
# even when the comparison's lower bound is negative. Do not claim positivity.
# A nonescaping integer result immediately consumed as a float needs no tagged
# temporary. The word arm still checks overflow; the cold arm computes the
# exact integer first and rounds it once, just like the ordinary conversion.
math_versioning = false

# Absolute addend bound for the narrow accumulator pattern: constants, the
# stable induction variable, sign changes, and a conditional choosing them.
# Distinct loop copies must not share scalar virtual registers: otherwise a
# later definition in the cold copy extends live intervals through hot calls.
# Bypass only the bundled default decimal formatter, following its checked
# forwarding wrappers. A user formatter or a format spec keeps its own call.
